+#
+# A blob that does not fit in one MQTT message is split, and the pieces are only
+# recognisable together. Observed on a Saros 10R fetching two obstacle photos
+# (`get_photo`, type 0, the whole camera frame):
+#
+#   64 KB frame  proto=301 seq=? rnd=?      ROBOROCK... payload_size=64413  -> complete
+#   88 KB frame  proto=300 seq=1 rnd=62633  ROBOROCK... payload_size=88001  -> 65232 bytes
+#                proto=301 seq=2 rnd=62633  <no header>                        22817 bytes
+#
+# So: protocol 300 marks the FIRST fragment of a fragmented blob, 301 is a
+# single-frame blob or a continuation, `random` is shared by the fragments of one
+# blob and `seq` numbers them from 1. Upstream only accepted 301 and required the
+# declared payload to fit inside that one message, so the 88 KB photo was dropped
+# on the floor and its caller timed out -- which read as "the vacuum no longer has
+# this photo" when the vacuum was in fact sending it.
